## Claim
Text: "Some US mayors are fining people for going to church during the COVID-19 pandemic."
Claim date: September 01, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did specific US city mayors issue fines to individuals for attending religious services during the COVID-19 pandemic?
Answer: Based on the search result, the answer is no; fines were not issued merely for attending religious services, but rather as a potential consequence for violating social distancing guidelines after a warning. The article states that if the Louisville Metro Department of Public Health received complaints about guideline violations, it could investigate and, for repeat offenders, "fine the violator."

Source URL: https://www.courier-journal.com/story/news/2020/04/21/louisville-mayor-fire-christian-enter-agreement-end-lawsuit/2999818001/

### Under what legal authority, such as a state or local emergency public health order, did these mayors claim the power to fine people for attending religious gatherings?
Answer: The mayors claimed this authority under state and local emergency public health orders, specifically enforcing compliance with the "stay-at-home order." As stated in the search result, "The Chicago Police Department has been working to ensure full compliance with the (stay-at-home) order," and fines were issued to churches for violating social distancing rules as part of that enforcement.

Source URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20210624060531/https://www.chicagotribune.com/coronavirus/ct-coronavirus-chicago-churches-fines-lightfoot-willie-wilson-20200521-y4mcdqlydjehdkzjbeh7rmhc4e-story.html

### Can specific, documented instances be found where a resident of a US city received and had to pay a fine for attending a church service during the COVID-19 pandemic?
Answer: Yes, a documented instance exists in **Greenville, Mississippi**, where the city fined congregants of Temple Baptist Church **$500 each** for attending a drive-in Easter parking lot church service. According to the source, "the city fined congregants $500 each for attending a parking lot sermon they could listen to using their car radio." Attorney General William Barr and the DOJ intervened, arguing the city had unfairly "singled churches out" by allowing drive-in restaurants to operate while prohibiting drive-in church services, and a lawsuit was filed by the church and the Alliance Defending Freedom. While this confirms that fines were issued, the article does not make entirely clear whether the fines were ultimately collected or rescinded following the DOJ's intervention and the legal challenge.

Source URL: https://www.salon.com/2020/04/15/william-barr-intervenes-after-mississippi-mayor-restricts-easter-church-service-due-to-coronavirus/

### What was the dollar amount of the fines that were levied by US mayors against individuals for attending religious services during the pandemic?
Answer: The search result indicates that three Chicago churches were fined $500 each for holding services in violation of the stay-at-home order, with the fines issued by authorities in Mayor Lori Lightfoot's jurisdiction.

Source URL: https://chicago.suntimes.com/coronavirus/2020/5/20/21265832/willie-wilson-pay-fines-chicago-churches-held-services-stay-at-home-pritzker-coronavirus

### Were any fines issued by US mayors for attending religious services during the COVID-19 pandemic later challenged, overturned, or deemed unconstitutional by a court?
Answer: Yes, such fines and restrictions were challenged and overturned. For example, a federal judge overruled Louisville Mayor Greg Fischer's order to ban drive-in church services during Easter 2020, stating the mayor's decision was "stunning" and "'beyond all reason,' unconstitutional."

Source URL: https://abcnews.go.com/US/kentucky-governor-warns-worshipers-congregate-easter-weekend/story?id=70101091

### Did the actions taken by US mayors during the COVID-19 pandemic to restrict religious gatherings apply equally to all religious denominations and places of worship, or were they targeted?
Answer: According to the search result, the restrictions imposed by at least one US mayor were characterized as targeting religious gatherings specifically. In Greenville, Mississippi, Mayor Errick Simmons' order barred church services while permitting drive-in restaurants to operate. Attorney General William Barr stated that "the city appears to have thereby singled churches out as the only essential service...that may not operate despite following all CDC and state recommendations." The Justice Department argued that the city's prohibition was "neither neutral nor generally applicable," suggesting discriminatory treatment of religious institutions compared to similar nonreligious activities. However, the mayor denied targeting the church, blaming confusion over unclear state guidelines. Notably, the source does not address whether restrictions varied among different Christian denominations or between different faiths—only that churches were allegedly singled out compared to comparable secular gatherings.

Source URL: https://www.salon.com/2020/04/15/william-barr-intervenes-after-mississippi-mayor-restricts-easter-church-service-due-to-coronavirus/

### During which specific months or period in 2020 were US mayors most actively fining individuals for attending religious services?
Answer: Based on the search result, US mayors were most actively fining individuals for attending religious services in **April 2020**, as exemplified by the case in Greenville, Mississippi, where congregants were fined $500 each for attending a parking lot sermon around Easter.

